eturn codes (a connection-reset during reboot, and a chained diagnostic command whose final sub-command against a nonexistent /proc/sys/kernel/audit path failed) are expected artifacts of the reboot and read-only diagnostics, not state-changing failures. Lifecycle diffs (temp files, listening sockets) are simply artifacts of the reboot itself (new boot_id-named systemd-private dirs, sockets rebinding), not executor-created residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-388a7cb1c38f45b8","cmd-5d932137ea484ef0","cmd-5044d779ccd74c6d","cmd-0b2b437eb1e747ce","cmd-7a81f5ea17d04cc6","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"The executor correctly diagnosed the cloud-image grub.d override and placed audit=1 in GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX (not the overridden GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT), regenerated grub.cfg, and then performed a real reboot that directly demonstrates the required outcome: a changed boot_id, post-reboot /proc/cmdline containing audit=1, and dmesg/audit_enabled confirming the kernel audit subsystem is active, with the node returning to a healthy systemctl is-system-running=running state. This is a complete, directly observed before/during/after transition satisfying both the persistence requirement and the mandated restart-evidence protocol, with no hygiene concerns.","limitations":["The final diagnostic command (cmd-7a81f5ea17d04cc6) returned exit code 1, likely because /proc/sys/kernel/audit does not exist as a file on this kernel; this does not affect the /proc/cmdline and dmesg evidence which are unambiguous.","Cluster/data-state recovery checks are not applicable beyond systemd health since this is a single-node task with no clustered service under test."]},"ttft_ms":16712,"type":"result","duration_ms":50569,"uuid":"eb02e9db-2e89-4775-a00b-653c5fd339e7","ttft_stream_ms":738,"time_to_request_ms":39,"first_content_frame_ms":1122,"queued_turn_count":0}
